 ],
 "literal": "O gentle-natured female dancer! You, great fame with the ear / other than hearing, do not know seeing. / To see, if you greatly desired, your excellent / fragrance-growing hair, as the mountain wind strokes, / like a peacock with its plumage, walking gracefully, / rain-like generosity-possessing / chariot-lord Āy, to see, do go!",
 "translation": "O gentle-natured songstress! You have only heard of his great fame with your ears, but have never seen him. If you greatly desire to see him, go now, with the mountain wind stroking your excellent, fragrant, thick hair, walking gracefully like a peacock with its full plumage, to see Lord Āy of the chariots, whose generosity is like the rain!",
 "modern": "மென்மையான இயல்புடைய விறலியே! நீ அவனுடைய நல்ல புகழைச் செவியால் கேட்டிருக்கிறாயே தவிர, நேரில் கண்டறிய மாட்டாய். நீ அவனைக் காணப் பெரிதும் விரும்பினால், உன்னுடைய சிறந்த, நறுமணம் கமழும் கூந்தலை மலைக்காற்று அசைக்க, தோகையையுடைய மயிலைப் போல அழகாக நடந்து, மழையைப் போன்ற கொடையை உடைய, தேர்களைக் கொண்ட வேளிர்குலத் தலைவனாகிய ஆயைக் காண்பதற்குச் செல்வாயாக!",
 "explanation": "The poet, Uṟaiyūr Ēṇiccēri Muṭamōciyār, is addressing a female dancer (viraḷi) in the theme of 'viraḷiyāṟṟuppaṭai' (guiding a female dancer to a patron). He tells her that she has only heard of the great fame of the chieftain Āy Aṇṭiraṉ, but has never seen him in person. He advises her that if she truly wishes to see him, she should set forth. He describes how she should walk gracefully like a peacock with its plumage spread, while the cool mountain breeze gently blows through her fragrant, thick hair. He praises Āy as a generous lord whose giving is like the life-giving rain, and urges her to go and receive his patronage.",
